    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ee8bc31a-d21a-5520-b1c9-d397836ee8dd",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{5t},\\,y=4{t}^{2}-8t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=5{e}^{5t},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=8t-8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t-8=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(1)=5{e}^{5}=5 e^{5}\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=1.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "afcde732-d4fd-5d99-bec4-a5da89e670fe",
