      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+3,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+4,\\,2\\pi\\le t<4\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{29\\pi}{12}) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{35\\pi}{12}) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{41\\pi}{12}) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{47\\pi}{12}) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.$",
