      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cc496cc2-5b68-5fb6-ab6a-0e20b6873203",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-10,\\,y=1\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+0,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-4\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{17\\pi}{6}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\frac{10\\pi}{3}) & =-4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
